  "content": {
    "text": "What is Elon Musk's net worth?\nWhen you buy through links on our articles, Future and its syndication partners may earn a commission.\nElon Musk often makes headlines – whether it's for rebranding Twitter as X, his brief political stint in Trump's administration or SpaceX's highly-anticipated IPO towards the end of this week.\nMusk has been the richest person in the world since 2021 – except for once when Oracle's Larry Ellison surpassed him in September 2025.\nElon Musk's net worth over the years\nElon Musk's net worth is $716 billion, according to the Bloomberg Billionaires Index. His wealth is more than twice that of Larry Page, the second-richest person and co-founder of Google. Page has a fortune of $309 billion.\nMusk's net worth has grown substantially over the past few years, driven largely by his stakes in Tesla and SpaceX.\nIn 2016, he was worth $10.7 billion. Just a decade later, he has surpassed the $700 billion mark – a feat no one has achieved before.\nBut where did it all begin?\nMusk was born in South Africa on 28 June 1971. From an early age, he showed an interest in technology and innovation.\nHis first major success came with Zip2, a company that provided online business directories and maps to newsletters. In 1999, Compaq acquired Zip2 for nearly $300 million, paving the way for Musk into the tech industry.\nMusk used proceeds from the sale to launch X.com, an online payment platform that eventually evolved into PayPal. In 2002, eBay acquired PayPal for $1.5 billion.\nThat same year, Musk used $100 million of his own fortune to start SpaceX, aiming to make space exploration more accessible and colonise Mars.\nIn June 2026, SpaceX is set to go public at $135 per share. The firm is expected to raise approximately $75 billion, which could make SpaceX the latest initial public offering of all time.\nIn 2004, Musk joined Tesla Motors and became the company's CEO four years later. Under his leadership, Tesla emerged as one of the world's most electric vehicle companies. At the time of writing, Tesla has a market capitalisation of $1.28 trillion.\nBeyond Tesla and SpaceX, Musk co-founded solar panel installation firm SolarCity, neurotechnology company Neuralink and tunnel construction firm The Boring Company.\nElon Musk's other investments\nWhile the majority of Musk's wealth comes from his business ventures, his influence stretches far beyond the tech world.\nHe was a prominent Donald Trump supporter during the 2024 US elections, and later served as the leader of the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), though he has since departed DOGE to focus on Tesla.",
